// What a range loop asks of a type, and the hierarchy of positions.
//
// A trait is a named set of *requirements*: operations that must compile for a
// type, written as expressions against a parameter. A type models the trait
// exactly when every requirement compiles with the parameter standing for it --
// there is no list of which types model what, because the answer is a question
// about the type rather than a claim about it.
//
// The chain below is the classical one: each trait adds one ability to the one
// above it. A position that can be read, moved forward, and compared for
// inequality is an `InputIterator`; one that can also be copied and re-compared
// is a `ForwardIterator`; and so on up to `RandomAccessIterator`, which can be
// moved by a count rather than one step at a time.
//
// There is no `ContiguousIterator`. Adjacency is a claim about *addresses*
// rather than about the position: `it + 1` being the next element in memory
// cannot be written as an operation, because every operation a position has
// works the same whether the elements are adjacent or not. C++20 states it with
// `to_address`, a function about the storage rather than about the position. A
// trait that only says "and also this, which I cannot check" would be a name
// that answers yes for anything a pointer happened to satisfy, so it is not
// here.
//
// "Adds to the one above it" is written the way every other requirement is
// written: a question asked of the same parameter, `InputIterator(it) -> true`.
// There is no inheritance clause, and none is needed -- a requirement another
// trait holds is a requirement like any other.
//
// Nothing here is emitted: a trait describes what a type can already do.

// Something bounded by `begin()` and `end()`.
//
// Both must yield something, because the range loop compares them: a `void`
// `end()` would leave the loop with nothing to test against. What they yield is
// an iterator, which is what the traits below name -- so that is a requirement
// too, written as a question: `begin()`'s type must model `InputIterator`.
export traits Iterable(it Iter) {
    it.begin() -> !void,
    it.end() -> !void,
    InputIterator(type(it.begin())) -> true,
}

// A position in a sequence that can be walked once.
//
// The three operations are the ones a loop performs on the position it holds:
// it moves to the next element, reads the current one, and stops when it has
// reached the end. Written against `it` as a *place*, which is why `++it` is
// legal here: `__inc__` takes `&this` and modifies the receiver.
export traits InputIterator(it Iter) {
    ++it,
    *it -> !void,
    it != it -> bool,
}

// A position that can be walked more than once.
//
// What separates it from an input iterator is that two positions can be
// compared for *equality* as well as inequality, and that a position can be
// copied by `it++` while the copy stays usable -- so a sequence may be walked
// twice.
export traits ForwardIterator(it Iter) {
    it == it -> bool,
    it++ -> !void,
    InputIterator(it) -> true,
}

// A position that can also go backwards.
export traits BidirectionalIterator(it Iter) {
    --it,
    ForwardIterator(it) -> true,
}

// A position that can be moved by an arbitrary count in one step.
//
// The offset is a `usize`, the same one indexing takes, so the literal in
// `it + 1` is what the requirement uses: it adopts the offset's type at the
// call site, exactly as `it[1]` does. `+=` and `-=` are required as well,
// because they are the form a walk uses -- `it += n` in one step rather than
// `n` increments.
export traits RandomAccessIterator(it Iter) {
    it + 1,
    it - 1,
    it += 1,
    it -= 1,
    it[1],
    it < it -> bool,
    BidirectionalIterator(it) -> true,
}
